VortX patch 0005: close CoreAudio init-failure listener lifetimes.

Base: mpv 8c67647b50059406c5c0444903597281b81516cf (unchanged).
Backports the intent of upstream fixes:
  af067b5ea8e5fe396ebd9d3f895e51a7e75b3c09 (cleanup ao on init failure)
  c5d391adba7bd024954d0df1e0405f5749f4d4ca (register after audiounit init)
  https://github.com/mpv-player/mpv/pull/18383

The generic AO owner calls driver uninit only after successful init. Registering
listeners before channel-map/AudioUnit setup therefore leaves a dangling AO
context when setup fails and mpv falls back to another output. The next device
notification reaches hotplug_cb -> mp_msg through freed memory (mpv#18274).

Also close adjacent failure edges exercised by our actual-source fixture:
  - clear a locally disposed AudioUnit before shared cleanup (no double disposal);
  - roll back only successfully added listeners if either registration fails,
    including the hotplug-only initializer (which also has no failure uninit);
  - keep zero/partial/repeated cleanup and registration refcounts balanced;
  - detach listeners before disposing resources inspected by their callback.

This is NOT a callback-queue redesign, leaked-context workaround, audio-format
override, or a whole-mpv upgrade. It does not claim to prove what triggered a
particular HAL notification or to solve an undocumented late callback after a
successful AudioObjectRemovePropertyListener. Removal errors remain logged.

Regression: python3 scripts/test-mpv-coreaudio-lifecycle.py
The fixture verifies SHA-pinned source, applies THIS patch, compiles the actual
lifecycle functions with ASan/UBSan and fake HAL calls, and requires baseline UAF
failures plus patched success. No app, playback, device mutation or provider use.

